;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; FAIL-WITH[--differentiate]: "only for the + reduction"
;; CHECK-FAIL: "only for the + reduction"
;; CHECK-FAIL-FLAGS: --hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv --differentiate
;;
;; FAIL-WITH[--differentiate] is not optional here, and its absence is only visible in the
;; --differentiate PHASE.  When the runner itself differentiates, the default pass becomes a
;; differentiating pass -- and this kernel is built to be refused there, so TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; alone reports the correct refusal as a failure.  FAIL-WITH says "expected to fail, with this
;; message" for that phase.  173-shuffles/errors/07 carries the same pair for the same reason.
;;
;; ENDEAVOUR 175 -- reduce-workgroup differentiates only the SUM reduction, and says why.
;;
;; TEST-EXPECT is PASS with CHECK-FAIL-FLAGS because the refusal is AD-only: the same kernel
;; compiles cleanly forward (spec 10 runs its twin on hardware), so the default pass must
;; succeed.  Contrast errors/01 and errors/03, whose refusals are backend-independent and which
;; therefore use TEST-EXPECT: FAIL.
;;
;; WHY THE LINE IS DRAWN AT +, and the reason is mathematical rather than a missing feature:
;;
;;   * SUM.  An all-reduce is a fan-in followed by a fan-out, so its transpose is a fan-out
;;     followed by a fan-in -- an all-reduce again.  Self-transposing, exact, and it needs
;;     NOTHING recorded from the forward pass.  That is the case spec 09 verifies at 64.0.
;;
;;   * MIN / MAX.  The adjoint routes to the thread that supplied the winning value and is zero
;;     everywhere else.  That is perfectly differentiable, but only if the forward pass STASHED
;;     the winner -- an argmin/argmax -- and it does not.  Ties need a subgradient convention
;;     too.  Deferred, not impossible.
;;
;;   * ARBITRARY BINOP.  Needs the partial derivatives of the operator at every combining node,
;;     which means the whole combining tree and its intermediates, i.e. a tape.  This is the case
;;     where the semantic shortcut genuinely does not rescue you.
;;
;; Refusing beats the alternative.  A reduction whose VJP quietly assumed + would return a
;; plausible number for a max reduction and be wrong, which is the failure mode this endeavour
;; already hit once: reversing reduce-workgroup's expansion mechanically gave analytical=1.0
;; against a true 64.0 with no error at all (BUG 073).
;;
;; CHECK-FAIL matches "only for the + reduction", a phrase that appears nowhere in the file name
;; or the kernel name -- CHECK-FAIL matches those too, so a carelessly named spec can satisfy its
;; own check without the compiler emitting anything.

(def-type mat-t (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-function wg-max (a b)
  (declare #'(float float => float))
  (if (> a b) a b))

(def-kernel maxed (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat-t &out mat-t)
           (global-size :set-to 64)
           (local-size  :set-to 64))
  (let ((sv (make-scratch-vector float 4)))
    (let ((tid (to-int (get-local-linear-id))))
      (let ((row (/ tid 16))
            (col (rem tid 16)))
        (let ((v (~ A row col)))
          (reduce-workgroup #'wg-max v -1.0e30 :local-scratch-vec sv)
          (set! (~ C row col) v))))))
